<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 10px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 2 – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Taking Care of Our Class</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Let's Arrange the Chairs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Extract specific information from short informational texts. Reading: Demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts. Phonemic Awareness: Decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of word parts, syllabification, and phonemic awareness.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can extract specific information from short informational texts about classroom chores (e.g., "We sweep the floor."). Can demonstrate comprehension of basic text descriptions by discussing what each chore means (e.g., "Sweep means clean the floor with a broom."). Can decode English graphemes and phonemes using knowledge of compound words and syllable division (e.g., recognizing "whiteboard" as "white-board" with two syllables).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards or images of various classroom chores (e.g., sweeping the floor, wiping the desks, arranging chairs, cleaning the whiteboard). Students identify the action in each picture. The teacher asks: "What is happening in this picture?" or "What chore is this?" focusing on previously introduced vocabulary (sweep, wipe, arrange, clean, chair, desk, whiteboard).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to look at a picture and guess the main idea. For example, pointing to an image of students arranging chairs: "They are putting the chairs in order. We arrange the chairs. The text we will read today is about arranging things and keeping our class tidy." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of /r/ in "arrange" and /s/ in "supplies" (linking to the previous lesson if applicable, or preparing for the upcoming text).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sweep' for cleaning the floor with a mop or a broom?" (Expected answer: "A broom.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If the classroom is messy, what do we do?" (Expected answer: "We clean up/tidy up/organize.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational text titled "Our Class Chores List" or "How We Keep Our Classroom Tidy." The text will describe basic classroom chores using imperative and present continuous forms, and incorporate the target vocabulary.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text (displayed on whiteboard or handout):<br>
                "**Our Daily Classroom Chores**<br>
                Welcome to our tidy classroom! Everyone helps to keep it clean and organized.<br>
                1. After class, please **arrange** the **chairs** neatly. This makes our room look **tidy**.<br>
                2. We **sweep** the floor **quickly** with a **dustpan** and broom. No dirt on the floor!<br>
                3. **Wipe** down the **desks** and **tables** with a **cloth** and **cleaner**. They are often a little **dirty**.<br>
                4. **Empty** the **trash can**. We are keeping our classroom super clean!<br>
                5. **Clean up** the **whiteboard**. It needs to be neat for the next lesson.<br>
                6. Can you help me put away the **cleaning supplies**? Thank you!"
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Extract specific information from short informational texts. Reading: Demonstrate comprehension of basic descriptions in texts.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Gist Question:** "What is this text mainly about?" (Expected: "Classroom chores" or "How to keep our class clean.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**True/False:** The teacher reads statements, and students say "True" or "False" based on the text.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We arrange the windows." (False)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We sweep the floor." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The desks are sometimes dirty." (True)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Reading)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading specific details from texts; using present continuous, imperatives, and modal "can"; recognizing vocabulary (nouns, verbs, adjectives, adverbs related to cleaning); decoding compound words and syllable division.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ol>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Reading for Specific Words (Controlled Practice):** Students receive a handout with the "Our Daily Classroom Chores" text. They underline all the **verbs** related to cleaning (sweep, wipe, arrange, empty, clean up, put away) in blue and all the **nouns** (chairs, floor, dustpan, broom, desks, tables, cloth, cleaner, trash can, whiteboard, cleaning supplies) in red. The teacher monitors and provides individual help.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Sentence Completion & Phonics Focus (Semi-controlled):** Students complete sentences based on the text, focusing on vocabulary and grammar. The teacher also highlights phonemic awareness.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We __________ the floor with a broom." (sweep)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Please __________ the chairs." (arrange)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you help me __________ the desks?" (wipe down)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The text says the whiteboard needs to be __________ for the next lesson." (neat)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Phonemic Awareness:** The teacher points to "whiteboard" in the text. "Look at 'whiteboard'. How many parts, or syllables, does it have? 'White-board'. Two syllables! It's a compound word." Students repeat "white-board" and identify other compound words if any, or practice syllable division for other multi-syllable words from the text (e.g., "clean-er," "or-gan-ized").</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Information Matching (Pair Check):** In pairs, students receive a set of short sentences or phrases describing a chore from the text (e.g., "Clean the floor with a broom," "Put the chairs in order," "Make the board ready for class"). They match each phrase to the correct chore number from the "Our Daily Classroom Chores" list. Partners check each other's answers by referring back to the original text. For example, Student A reads "Put the chairs in order," Student B finds "1. Please arrange the chairs neatly" and says "Number 1, arrange the chairs."</li>
            </ol>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Taking Care of Our Class")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Task Format:** "Classroom Chore Chart Creation."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students, in small groups (3-4), are given blank chore chart templates (a simple grid with days of the week and empty spaces for chores). They must re-read "Our Daily Classroom Chores" and, based on the information provided, write down one chore description per day for their group's assigned week. They can also read short additional chore descriptions (e.g., "Make sure the windows are clean," "Organize the books on the table") and decide where to place them on their chart. The focus is on reading comprehension to correctly assign and represent chores. They use the vocabulary from the text (e.g., "arrange the chairs," "sweep the floor," "wipe the desks"). The teacher encourages them to use polite language as learned: "Let's arrange the chairs," "Please wipe the desks."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Focus:** Fluency in understanding and applying the text information, collaborative interaction to complete the chart.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Formative Assessment Strategy:** Teacher observation checklist during group work and a short individual exit ticket.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Assessment Task:**</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Group Chore Chart:** The teacher observes if groups accurately extract information from the text to fill their chore charts.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">**Individual Exit Ticket:** Students receive a small slip with 2-3 short sentences. They must read each sentence and draw a simple picture representing the chore, or match it to a given word.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We are sweeping the floor." (Student draws a broom and dustpan.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you arrange the chairs, please?" (Student draws chairs being put in order.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Evaluation Criteria (for Exit Ticket):**</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in understanding the described action (e.g., drawing sweep vs. wipe).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognition of key vocabulary from the text (e.g., chair, floor, desk).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">**Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket Prompts:**</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about cleaning did you learn today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to read about the chores? Why?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one classroom chore you understand well after reading the text?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can reading a list of chores help us keep our classroom clean?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., students engaged with the text, correctly identified key verbs/nouns, practice activities were effective). What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., more visual support for new vocabulary, explicit teaching of sentence structure for descriptions, additional practice with compound words). Were students able to extract specific information from the text effectively? Was the phonemic awareness activity accessible?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction) will build on the comprehension of classroom chores and vocabulary from this reading lesson. Students will use the information from the chore list to talk about and assign chores to each other, practicing offering help and giving instructions verbally.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>